{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>תוסף</b> — compounded from יסף and ספה, which is ספות החטאת (the adding of [the words] of the sin), and the witness to it is the shva that is under the samekh. And another interpretation: and it is compounded from תוסף and from תוכף on the pattern of ירד. And now I return to interpret and say that the words of the sage are seven parts, all on the subject of reproof, and each part [has] four things — except 'two things I have asked,' and so too our Sages practiced in saying 'four temperaments in a person, four types among students.' And among these are six verses that adhere to the book of four elements, and they are: heaven — the element of fire, which is hot and dry; wind — the element of moisture and heat; water — cold and moist; earth — the element of cold and dryness. And so the interpretation: 'The words' stands in place of 'two,' as it were — the words of Agur, the words of the utterance and the prophecy, like the counsel of Ahithophel, 'when a man asks a matter of Elohim.' 'The utterance of the man' — perhaps Itiel and Ukal asked him matters of divine wisdom, and the man humbled himself and said to them: How do you ask me, for I am more brutish than a man, and I do not even have the understanding of a single man, and I have not learned wisdom concerning the divine, nor do I know the knowledge of El — that is, I have no wisdom or knowledge." "<b>פן יוכיח בך</b> — in the matter that one transgresses a negative commandment." "<b>ונכזבת</b> — you will be proved false by occupying yourself with a wisdom other than the wisdom of His commandments, and 'you will be proved false' as in בנים כחשים (Deut. 32:20), and they are so called for rebelling against the mouth of HaShem."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The long comment covers the lemma תוסף and then shifts to a general structural analysis of Proverbs 30; 'the sage' presumably refers to Agur. The reading 'the book of four elements' is literal; witnesses may differ. The clause 'the words stands in place of two' is hyper-literal; Ibn Ezra appears to argue that דברי (plural) implies a dual subject."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:25:37.651946615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}